// TEST: Group By Prints Its Rows
// A grouped array prints its ROWS, in both lanes. The compiled printer used to
// walk the value's placeholder inner extent 0 and print `g = [[], [], []]`;
// the interpreter printed the same thing for a direct group_by and the real
// rows through an alias, so `let h = g` disagreed between the notebook and
// `blade run`. The alias also COPIED as a dense rank-2 array of inner extent
// 0 in C++ -- an empty array a peel over h then read nothing from.
let region = [0, 1, 0, 2]
let temps = [10.0, 20.0, 30.0, 40.0]
let gk = group_keys(region)
let g = group_by(temps, gk)
// EXPECT: g = [[10.0, 30.0], [20.0], [40.0]]
let h = g
// EXPECT: h = [[10.0, 30.0], [20.0], [40.0]]
// The copy is a real grouped array: a peel over it reads its rows.
let sums = method_for(h) <@> lambda(r: Array<Float64 like RaggedIdx<_>>) -> reduce(r, (+)) |> compute
// EXPECT: sums = [40.0, 20.0, 40.0]
